    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.2.03-01",
      "block_type": "definition",
      "heading": "Demographic, social and economic effects",
      "text": "HIV-related illness and deaths can affect population structure and livelihoods where prevention, diagnosis, treatment or care are insufficient. The consequences are not fixed for every person living with HIV. Effective treatment can preserve health and participation, while social support and fair access can reduce avoidable hardship. A geographical explanation needs the condition and pathway rather than the unsupported statement that HIV status means inability to work.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-3.2.03-1",
        "CL-6137-3.2.03-2",
