      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "An age-structure claim needs an appropriate comparison. A record of deaths among adults can be relevant, but the total number living at those ages and other causes are needed to assess the scale. A large number of deaths can partly reflect a large population. Likewise, a lower observed death count after treatment expansion may be encouraging without isolating the programme's exact causal effect. Appropriate rates, methods and time records strengthen the interpretation.",
